{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Fins and scales</b> — סנפירים (fins) are that with which it swims; קשקשת (scales) are those fixed in it. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:9), and so it is in the Gemara in tractate Ḥullin (Ḥullin 59a). But do not understand from their language that they are literally fixed in its body and attached to the skin of the fish; rather, they called them \"fixed\" (קבועים) because they do not move about on it and do not quiver on it as fins do. They are the round peelings whose scale-like covering resembles a fingernail, which peel off from the skin of the fish by hand or with a knife. But anything that is fixed and attached to the skin of the fish and does not separate from the skin at all is not a קשקשת (scale), and its possessor is forbidden. Therefore they said in the Gemara (ibid. 66b): \"A scale is a garment.\" And the language of the Tosefta (Ḥullin 3:9): \"These are the scales with which it is clothed, and fins with which it swims.\" And this is the translation of Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 11:9), who said קלפין, meaning they are peelings upon the skin that peel off and are peeled from it like the peelings of trees and fruits, in keeping with the verse that says (I Samuel 17:5): \"And a coat of scales (קשקשים) he was wearing\" — for all coats of mail are made of rings, and sometimes they make coverings over them to plug the openings of the rings so that a thin arrow will not penetrate them, and those coverings are called קשקשים (scales). Therefore the Sages in the Gemara in chapter Ḥeleq (Sanhedrin 95a) mentioned \"coverings of mail, a covering (קלפה).\" And Jonathan ben Uzziel translated: ושריון גלבין, from the expression תער הגלבים, \"the razor of the barbers\" (Ezekiel 5:1) — these are the leather-workers. He intends to say that the covering over the opening of the ring of that coat of mail was like the peelings of fish skin, for they tan tough hide and cover the coats of mail with it, as is done even today. And understand this well.\n\nAnd the reason for the fin and the scale is that their possessors dwell perpetually in the upper waters and in their clear depths, and they receive nourishment from the air that enters there. Therefore they possess a certain warmth that repels from them the excess of moisture, just as wool and hair, and likewise the nails in humans and animals, do. But that which has no fin and scale dwells perpetually in the lowest waters and in their murky depths, and because of the great moisture and the lack of warmth, it repels nothing. Therefore they are creatures of cold, clammy moisture close to lethal, and it is deadly in some waters such as stagnant marshes."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The citation to Sanhedrin 95a is likely a reference to the phrase קלפה appearing in that context; the precise referent within the chapter is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:51.107827719Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}